###Pure

// ============================================================
// client-portal (L3) -- what a client actually sees
// ============================================================
// Depends on client-reporting and fee-billing, and on nothing else.
//
// FOUR LEVELS FROM THE INSTRUMENT MASTER. A holding line on a portal screen is
//
//    core-instrument (L0) -> position-keeping (L1) -> client-reporting (L2) -> here (L3)
//
// and the whole point of this project is that it does not restate a single number from any of
// those four levels. A portal is NOT the reports. The reports already exist upstream, priced,
// totalled and delivered. What is missing above them is everything that decides whether a
// particular human, logged in from a particular device on a particular Tuesday, is shown a
// particular document at all -- and the record of what they were in fact shown, which is the
// only thing a complaint can be answered from two years later.
//
// So this project owns six things and none of them are numbers:
//
//    ENTITLEMENT     who may see whose statements, at what depth, and until when
//    SESSION         the logged-in stretch inside which everything else happened
//    NAVIGATION      the menu tree, and which permission each branch needs
//    ACKNOWLEDGEMENT the documents that must be accepted before the portal moves on
//    OUTSTANDING     the invoices shown as owing, from fee-billing's side
//    AUDIT           what was rendered, to whom, when, and against which text
//
// THE DIAMOND. `client_reporting::Store` includes `client_core::Store`, and so does
// `fee_billing::Store`. Including both here reaches client-core by two routes and it resolves
// to ONE -- verified before anything else in this project was written, because the failure
// mode would have surfaced sixteen classes in. See MANIFEST.md.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT NAMED. client-core, position-keeping, valuation-core, fee-core,
// core-party, core-geo, core-tenor and the rest of the closure are all on the classpath and
// would COMPILE if named. None of them is a declared dependency of this project, so every
// client id, mandate id and contact id below is a plain `String` carried on this project's
// own rows rather than a join into a project this one did not declare. Where a name from
// client-core is genuinely wanted, it is one more hop off a client-reporting object that
// already reaches it: `$entry.statement.clientName`.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the identity
// FOR: a login, which is NOT a client. One human may act for four clients (a trustee), and
// one client may have nine logins (a family office's staff). Keeping the identity separate
// from the entitlement is what makes revoking one relationship possible without deleting a
// person, and it is why `CpoUserEntitlement` below is a table rather than a column here.
Class client_portal::CpoPortalUser
{
   userId: String[1];
   loginName: String[1];
   displayName: String[1];
   emailAddress: String[0..1];
   // CLIENT, ADVISER, INTERMEDIARY, STAFF. A STAFF login sees the portal as the client sees
   // it, which is why impersonation has to be visible in the audit trail rather than
   // indistinguishable from the client's own reading.
   userType: String[1];
   // PENDING, ACTIVE, SUSPENDED, CLOSED.
   status: String[1];
   enrolledOn: StrictDate[1];
   // Null until the first successful login. A never-logged-in user with statements waiting is
   // a delivery failure that no delivery record shows.
   lastLoginAt: DateTime[0..1];
   // Null unless locked out. A date, not a flag, because how long they were locked out is the
   // question that gets asked.
   lockedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   // SMS, TOTP, PUSH, NONE.
   mfaMethod: String[0..1];
   // BCP 47. Chooses the rendering, and must be reconcilable with the template language a
   // statement was actually produced in upstream.
   preferredLanguage: String[0..1];

   isActive() { $this.lockedOn->isEmpty() && $this.status == 'ACTIVE' } : Boolean[1];
   // A login that exists and has never been used. The statements went out; nobody opened them.
   hasNeverLoggedIn() { $this.lastLoginAt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isStaff() { $this.userType == 'STAFF' } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- who may see what
// FOR: the grant that lets one login see one client's (or one mandate's) material, at one
// depth. THE class of this project: every screen in the portal is a filter over these rows,
// and a document shown outside them is the incident this whole model exists to make
// answerable. Dated on both sides, because "was she entitled to see it ON THE DAY" is the
// question, and revoking a grant must not rewrite what was legitimately shown last March.
Class client_portal::CpoUserEntitlement
{
   entitlementId: String[1];
   userId: String[1];
   // client-core's client id, carried as a String. client-core is NOT a dependency of this
   // project; the same id reached through `$entry.statement.client` is one hop away for a
   // caller that wants the object.
   clientId: String[1];
   // Null means the whole relationship. Set means this mandate only, which is how a trustee
   // is given one portfolio out of six.
   mandateId: String[0..1];
   // PORTFOLIO, BILLING, DOCUMENTS, ALL. The billing scope is separable because the person
   // who reads the invoices is frequently not the person who reads the valuations.
   scope: String[1];
   // VIEW, DOWNLOAD, ACKNOWLEDGE, INSTRUCT. Ordered, and `rank()` below is what a screen
   // compares against a navigation node's requirement.
   permissionLevel: String[1];
   grantedOn: StrictDate[1];
   grantedBy: String[1];
   // MANDATE, POWER_OF_ATTORNEY, STAFF_ROLE, CLIENT_DELEGATION -- what the grant rests on.
   sourceKind: String[1];
   // Null while live. Not a delete: a revoked grant is the evidence that access was correct
   // at the time and stopped afterwards.
   revokedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   isLive() { $this.revokedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Whole-relationship rather than one portfolio.
   coversWholeRelationship() { $this.mandateId->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // The ladder, as an Integer, so a node requirement can be compared numerically.
   rank()
   {
      if($this.permissionLevel == 'INSTRUCT', | 4,
         | if($this.permissionLevel == 'ACKNOWLEDGE', | 3,
              | if($this.permissionLevel == 'DOWNLOAD', | 2, | 1)))
   } : Integer[1];
   canAcknowledge() { $this.rank() >= 3 } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the logged-in stretch
// FOR: one continuous visit. Everything the portal did is hung off a session rather than off
// a user, because "she saw it" is weaker evidence than "she saw it at 14:02 from this device
// in this session that had passed MFA", and the second is what a regulator asks for.
Class client_portal::CpoSession
{
   sessionId: String[1];
   userId: String[1];
   startedAt: DateTime[1];
   // Null while the session is open.
   endedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   // LOGOUT, TIMEOUT, REVOKED, EXPIRED. Kept because a timeout mid-acknowledgement is a
   // different story from a logout after one.
   endReason: String[0..1];
   ipAddress: String[0..1];
   userAgent: String[0..1];
   // WEB, MOBILE_APP, TABLET.
   deviceType: String[0..1];
   mfaPassed: Boolean[1];
   // If a STAFF login is acting as a client, this is the client they are acting as. Null
   // otherwise, and non-null is what separates the client's own reading from ours.
   impersonatedClientId: String[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.endedAt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isImpersonation() { $this.impersonatedClientId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // A session that ended by itself rather than by the user.
   timedOut() { $this.endReason == 'TIMEOUT' } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the menu
// FOR: one branch of the portal's navigation tree -- the thing a client clicks. Modelled
// because the tree is not cosmetic: a document that exists, is entitled and is never reachable
// from any node was, in practice, not shown. The parent link is a SELF-JOIN on this table.
Class client_portal::CpoNavigationNode
{
   nodeCode: String[1];
   // Null at the root. The tree is one table, walked by {target} in the store.
   parentNodeCode: String[0..1];
   label: String[1];
   // SECTION, DOCUMENT_LIST, INVOICE_LIST, ALERT_LIST, ACTION.
   nodeType: String[1];
   displayOrder: Integer[1];
   routePath: String[0..1];
   // The node the client lands on after login. Exactly one should be true; nothing here
   // enforces that, which is itself worth knowing.
   isLanding: Boolean[1];
   // Null while the node is in the menu.
   retiredOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   isRoot() { $this.parentNodeCode->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isLive() { $this.retiredOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- what a branch demands
// FOR: the permission a navigation node requires before it renders. Separate from the node so
// one branch can demand different things of different scopes -- the invoice list needs
// BILLING/VIEW, the pay button needs BILLING/INSTRUCT, and they are the same screen.
Class client_portal::CpoNodeAccess
{
   nodeAccessId: String[1];
   nodeCode: String[1];
   // Compared against CpoUserEntitlement.rank().
   requiredPermission: String[1];
   // PORTFOLIO, BILLING, DOCUMENTS, ALL.
   requiredScope: String[1];
   // Whether a denied node disappears or greys out. Greyed out tells the client the feature
   // exists and they lack it; hidden tells them nothing. Both are defensible and the choice
   // is per node.
   hideWhenDenied: Boolean[1];
   effectiveFrom: StrictDate[1];

   // The numeric requirement, matched against an entitlement's rank.
   requiredRank()
   {
      if($this.requiredPermission == 'INSTRUCT', | 4,
         | if($this.requiredPermission == 'ACKNOWLEDGE', | 3,
              | if($this.requiredPermission == 'DOWNLOAD', | 2, | 1)))
   } : Integer[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the catalogue row
// FOR: a document as it appears in the portal's list -- title, date, size, and whether it can
// be taken away. It is NOT the statement. `client_reporting::CrpStatement` is the statement;
// this row is the portal's decision to publish it, which happens later, can be reversed, and
// carries the rendered hash of the file actually served. A statement with no entry was
// produced and never shown.
Class client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry
{
   entryId: String[1];
   // The upstream statement. Nullable because an entry can also be a portal-only document --
   // a terms update, a fee schedule -- that no statement run produced.
   statementId: String[0..1];
   // The dispatch this entry corresponds to, where the document also went out on a channel.
   deliveryId: String[0..1];
   clientId: String[1];
   // Where in the menu it hangs.
   nodeCode: String[0..1];
   // What the client reads in the list. NOT derived from the statement type: the portal's
   // wording and the document's wording drift, and the list is what they saw.
   title: String[1];
   // STATEMENT, DISCLOSURE, NOTICE, INVOICE, CONTRACT, CORRESPONDENCE.
   documentKind: String[1];
   publishedAt: DateTime[1];
   // Null means it stays. Set means it drops off the list, without ceasing to have been shown.
   expiresOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   fileSizeBytes: Integer[0..1];
   isDownloadable: Boolean[1];
   // Of the file actually served. Compared against `CrpDelivery.documentHash` this answers
   // "was the portal serving the same PDF that was emailed", and a mismatch is a real defect
   // that nothing upstream can see.
   renderedHash: String[0..1];
   // Null while published. Set when pulled -- a withdrawal upstream should land here too, and
   // whether it did is checkable.
   unpublishedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   isPublished() { $this.unpublishedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // A portal-only document with no upstream statement behind it.
   isPortalOnly() { $this.statementId->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the file served matches the file dispatched. Written with ->exists so an absent
   // delivery is FALSE rather than empty: a Boolean[1] that has to answer "did they match"
   // must answer no when there is nothing to match against.
   matchesDispatchedFile()
   {
      $this.delivery->exists(d | $d.documentHash == $this.renderedHash)
   } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- what was actually shown
// FOR: one opening of one document by one user in one session. THE audit row. Everything else
// in this project can be reconstructed; this cannot, and it is the row a complaint is answered
// from -- not "the statement was sent" but "it was opened on the 14th, eleven of its twelve
// pages were reached, and it was not downloaded".
Class client_portal::CpoDocumentView
{
   viewId: String[1];
   entryId: String[1];
   sessionId: String[1];
   userId: String[1];
   openedAt: DateTime[1];
   // The DATE of openedAt, stamped on the row. A ~groupBy takes column REFERENCES and not
   // expressions, so a view that groups by day needs the day to exist as a column.
   openedDate: StrictDate[1];
   secondsOnPage: Integer[0..1];
   pagesSeen: Integer[0..1];
   wasDownloaded: Boolean[1];
   // The hash of what was rendered AT THIS VIEW. Compared against the entry's, it catches a
   // document that was quietly replaced between two readings of it.
   renderedHash: String[0..1];
   // Which menu branch they arrived from. An alert-driven read and a browse are different
   // evidence of whether the client was told.
   sourceNodeCode: String[0..1];

   // Opened rather than merely listed, and long enough to have been read at all.
   wasEngaged() { $this.secondsOnPage->orElse(0) > 5 } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether this reading saw the same bytes the catalogue claims to hold.
   hashAgrees() { $this.entry->exists(e | $e.renderedHash == $this.renderedHash) } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- awaiting acceptance
// FOR: a document the client must accept before the portal lets them past -- a costs
// disclosure, a terms change, a risk warning. The REQUEST is separate from the receipt below
// because an unanswered request is the interesting one: it is the queue, and its age is the
// number that gets reported.
Class client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementRequest
{
   requestId: String[1];
   entryId: String[1];
   // The upstream costs-and-charges disclosure, where that is what must be accepted. Null for
   // a request over a portal-only document.
   disclosureId: String[0..1];
   clientId: String[1];
   raisedOn: StrictDate[1];
   // The date by which acceptance is required.
   requiredBy: StrictDate[1];
   // REGULATORY, CONTRACTUAL, ADVISORY -- why acceptance is being demanded, because only the
   // first two justify blocking.
   reasonCode: String[1];
   // Whether the portal refuses to move on without it.
   isBlocking: Boolean[1];
   // Null unless chased.
   escalatedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   // Null unless withdrawn. A cancelled request was never answered and must not be counted as
   // outstanding OR as satisfied.
   cancelledOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   isCancelled() { $this.cancelledOn->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Live and unanswered: the queue.
   isOutstanding() { $this.cancelledOn->isEmpty() && $this.receipt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   wasEscalated() { $this.escalatedOn->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the acceptance
// FOR: the click that answered a request. At most one per request, so `requestId` is the whole
// key. `statementTextHash` is the load-bearing column: it records WHICH WORDING was accepted,
// which is the difference between an acknowledgement and an assertion that one happened.
Class client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementReceipt
{
   requestId: String[1];
   userId: String[1];
   sessionId: String[1];
   acceptedAt: DateTime[1];
   // Of the text on the screen at the moment of acceptance.
   statementTextHash: String[1];
   ipAddress: String[0..1];
   // Whether the client reached the end of the text before the button enabled. False is not
   // fatal and is exactly what a challenge turns on.
   wasScrolledToEnd: Boolean[1];
   // TICKBOX, BUTTON, TYPED_NAME, WET_SIGNATURE_UPLOAD.
   acceptanceMethod: String[1];

   // Accepted by a login that was standing in for the client rather than by the client.
   byStaffProxy() { $this.acceptingUser->exists(u | $u.isStaff()) } : Boolean[1];
   // The strong form: read to the end and accepted deliberately.
   isInformed() { $this.wasScrolledToEnd && $this.acceptanceMethod != 'TICKBOX' } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- money on the screen
// FOR: an invoice as the PORTAL shows it. fee-billing owns what is owed; this row owns what
// was displayed and when it became visible, which are different dates and occasionally
// different numbers. `amountShown` is stored rather than read through the join on purpose: if
// it ever disagrees with `BilInvoice.chargeAfterLimits()` that disagreement is a finding, and
// a derived property would have hidden it.
Class client_portal::CpoInvoiceView
{
   portalInvoiceId: String[1];
   // fee-billing's invoice.
   invoiceId: String[1];
   clientId: String[1];
   // The catalogue row the PDF hangs off, where one was published.
   entryId: String[0..1];
   publishedAt: DateTime[1];
   // DUE, OVERDUE, PAID, DISPUTED, CANCELLED -- the portal's word for it, which is softer than
   // the biller's status and is what the client reads.
   displayStatus: String[1];
   // What the screen said. Compare with the join, do not replace it.
   amountShown: Float[1];
   // What is still owing on it.
   outstandingAmount: Float[1];
   currencyCode: String[1];
   dueDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Null until someone opens it. The portal's own read receipt for a bill.
   firstOpenedAt: DateTime[0..1];

   isSettled() { $this.outstandingAmount <= 0.0 } : Boolean[1];
   hasBeenOpened() { $this.firstOpenedAt->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // What the portal displayed against what the biller computed. Nothing forces these to
   // agree, and that is the point of storing both.
   agreesWithBiller() { $this.invoice->exists(i | $i.chargeAfterLimits() == $this.amountShown) } : Boolean[1];
   // The share still owing, as a fraction. Integer/Integer widens, but these are already
   // Float, and the guard is against a zero invoice rather than against typing.
   settledFraction()
   {
      if($this.amountShown == 0.0, | 1.0, | 1.0 - ($this.outstandingAmount / $this.amountShown))
   } : Float[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the client acting
// FOR: the client telling the portal to pay an invoice. The portal does not settle anything --
// `settledOn` is stamped when the payment rail confirms -- but the INSTRUCTION is the client's
// act and belongs here rather than in the biller, which never sees a screen.
Class client_portal::CpoPaymentInstruction
{
   instructionId: String[1];
   portalInvoiceId: String[1];
   // Carried as well as the portal id, so an instruction can be reconciled against fee-billing
   // without going through this project's own row.
   invoiceId: String[1];
   userId: String[1];
   sessionId: String[1];
   instructedAt: DateTime[1];
   amount: Float[1];
   currencyCode: String[1];
   // DIRECT_DEBIT, TRANSFER, CARD, DEDUCT_FROM_PORTFOLIO. The last one is why this cannot be a
   // pure billing concept: it moves the client's own assets.
   method: String[1];
   reference: String[0..1];
   // Null until the rail confirms.
   settledOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   rejectedReason: String[0..1];

   isSettled() { $this.settledOn->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   wasRejected() { $this.rejectedReason->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Paying less than the screen said is owing. A part payment is not a rejection and is not a
   // settlement, and conflating the three is how disputes start.
   isPartPayment() { $this.portalInvoice->exists(v | $this.amount < $v.outstandingAmount) } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the nag
// FOR: something the portal is putting in front of the client -- a notice with a deadline, an
// invoice gone overdue, a disclosure unaccepted, a dispute answered. An alert can point at an
// upstream notice OR at an upstream billing dispute OR at a local document entry; all three
// links are nullable and an alert with none of them is a plain message.
Class client_portal::CpoAlert
{
   alertId: String[1];
   clientId: String[1];
   // Null for a client-wide alert shown to every entitled login.
   userId: String[0..1];
   // client-reporting's ten-percent depreciation notice, where that is what raised this.
   noticeId: String[0..1];
   // fee-billing's dispute, where the alert is telling the client what happened to it.
   disputeId: String[0..1];
   entryId: String[0..1];
   // DOCUMENT_READY, ACK_REQUIRED, INVOICE_OVERDUE, DEPRECIATION_NOTICE, DISPUTE_RESOLVED,
   // ACCESS_CHANGED.
   alertType: String[1];
   // INFO, WARNING, ACTION_REQUIRED, CRITICAL.
   severity: String[1];
   raisedAt: DateTime[1];
   expiresOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   // Null while it is still on screen.
   dismissedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   message: String[1];

   isLive() { $this.dismissedAt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   needsAction() { $this.severity == 'ACTION_REQUIRED' || $this.severity == 'CRITICAL' } : Boolean[1];
   // Raised by a regulatory deadline upstream rather than by the portal itself.
   isRegulatory() { $this.noticeId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the trail
// FOR: everything the portal did, whether or not it succeeded. Wider than CpoDocumentView,
// which records only successful renders: a DENIED event -- someone reached for a document they
// were not entitled to -- has no view row and is precisely what an access review looks for.
Class client_portal::CpoAuditEvent
{
   eventId: String[1];
   sessionId: String[1];
   userId: String[1];
   eventAt: DateTime[1];
   // The DATE, stamped on the row so the daily view can group on a column reference.
   eventDate: StrictDate[1];
   // LOGIN, LOGOUT, NAVIGATE, VIEW_DOCUMENT, DOWNLOAD, ACKNOWLEDGE, INSTRUCT_PAYMENT,
   // DENIED, MFA_CHALLENGE, EXPORT.
   eventType: String[1];
   nodeCode: String[0..1];
   // ENTRY, INVOICE, REQUEST, NODE, SESSION -- what kind of thing targetId names. A loose
   // pointer on purpose: the alternative is eleven nullable foreign keys.
   targetKind: String[0..1];
   targetId: String[0..1];
   // OK, DENIED, ERROR, TIMEOUT.
   outcome: String[1];
   durationMs: Integer[0..1];
   detail: String[0..1];

   wasDenied() { $this.outcome == 'DENIED' } : Boolean[1];
   isReadOfDocument() { $this.eventType == 'VIEW_DOCUMENT' || $this.eventType == 'DOWNLOAD' } : Boolean[1];
}

// ================================================================ the aggregate classes
//
// Rooted on store VIEWS. One instance is a GROUP, never a row: the set's primary key is the
// view's ~groupBy and nothing else. An empty group produces NO ROW -- an entry nobody has ever
// opened is ABSENT from CpoEntryViewTotal rather than present with a count of zero, which is
// why "never opened" has to be read from the entry side.

// FOR: a document read as one row -- how many times it was opened, by how many sessions, and
// when it was first and last reached. This is the answer to "did the client ever look at it",
// and it is a GROUP, so it is absent rather than zero when they never did.
Class client_portal::CpoEntryViewTotal
{
   entryId: String[1];
   viewCount: Integer[1];
   totalSeconds: Integer[1];
   totalPagesSeen: Integer[1];
   firstOpenedDate: StrictDate[1];
   lastOpenedDate: StrictDate[1];

   // Integer / Integer widens to Float on its own, which is why this is Float[1].
   averageSecondsPerView() { $this.totalSeconds / $this.viewCount } : Float[1];
   // Opened more than once. A second reading is usually a client who did not understand the
   // first one.
   wasRevisited() { $this.viewCount > 1 } : Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: one login's activity on one day -- the row an access review reads. The grain is the
// PAIR, so an instance is a (user, day) and never an event.
Class client_portal::CpoUserActivityTotal
{
   userId: String[1];
   eventDate: StrictDate[1];
   eventCount: Integer[1];
   totalDurationMs: Integer[1];

   averageDurationMs() { $this.totalDurationMs / $this.eventCount } : Float[1];
   // A day busy enough to be worth reading the detail of.
   isHeavyDay() { $this.eventCount > 100 } : Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: what one client owes across every invoice on their screen, with the mandate and the
// billing run both DROPPED by the grouping. This is the number the portal's landing page
// prints, and reconstructing it from `fee_billing::BilRunTotal` would give a different answer,
// because a run is not a client.
Class client_portal::CpoClientOutstandingTotal
{
   clientId: String[1];
   invoiceCount: Integer[1];
   totalOutstanding: Float[1];
   totalShown: Float[1];
   latestDueDate: StrictDate[1];

   // The share of what was billed that is still owing.
   outstandingFraction()
   {
      if($this.totalShown == 0.0, | 0.0, | $this.totalOutstanding / $this.totalShown)
   } : Float[1];
   isClear() { $this.totalOutstanding <= 0.0 } : Boolean[1];
}

// ================================================================ associations, internal
//
// Twenty-one of them. Every one is an Association rather than a one-directional property,
// because the portal is navigated from both ends: from the user down ("what may she see") and
// from the document up ("who saw this"), and the second direction is the one an investigation
// uses.

Association client_portal::CpoUserEntitlements
{
   entitledUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   entitlements: client_portal::CpoUserEntitlement[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoUserSessions
{
   sessionUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   sessions: client_portal::CpoSession[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoNodeParent
{
   parentNode: client_portal::CpoNavigationNode[0..1];
   childNodes: client_portal::CpoNavigationNode[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoNodeAccessRules
{
   accessNode: client_portal::CpoNavigationNode[0..1];
   accessRules: client_portal::CpoNodeAccess[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoEntryNode
{
   entryNode: client_portal::CpoNavigationNode[0..1];
   nodeEntries: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoEntryViews
{
   entry: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[0..1];
   views: client_portal::CpoDocumentView[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoSessionViews
{
   viewSession: client_portal::CpoSession[0..1];
   documentViews: client_portal::CpoDocumentView[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoUserViews
{
   viewingUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   userViews: client_portal::CpoDocumentView[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoEntryAckRequests
{
   requestedEntry: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[0..1];
   acknowledgementRequests: client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementRequest[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoRequestReceipt
{
   request: client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementRequest[0..1];
   receipt: client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementReceipt[0..1];
}

Association client_portal::CpoReceiptUser
{
   acceptingUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   receipts: client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementReceipt[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoReceiptSession
{
   receiptSession: client_portal::CpoSession[0..1];
   sessionReceipts: client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementReceipt[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoInvoiceViewEntry
{
   invoiceEntry: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[0..1];
   invoiceViews: client_portal::CpoInvoiceView[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoInvoicePayments
{
   portalInvoice: client_portal::CpoInvoiceView[0..1];
   instructions: client_portal::CpoPaymentInstruction[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoInstructionUser
{
   instructingUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   userInstructions: client_portal::CpoPaymentInstruction[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoAlertUser
{
   alertedUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   alerts: client_portal::CpoAlert[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoAlertEntry
{
   alertEntry: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[0..1];
   entryAlerts: client_portal::CpoAlert[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoSessionAudit
{
   auditSession: client_portal::CpoSession[0..1];
   auditEvents: client_portal::CpoAuditEvent[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoUserAudit
{
   auditUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   userAuditEvents: client_portal::CpoAuditEvent[*];
}

// The three into this project's own aggregates. The end on the aggregate side is [0..1]
// because an empty group is no row at all.
Association client_portal::CpoEntryViewTotals
{
   totalEntry: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[0..1];
   viewTotal: client_portal::CpoEntryViewTotal[0..1];
}

Association client_portal::CpoUserActivityTotals
{
   activityUser: client_portal::CpoPortalUser[0..1];
   activityTotals: client_portal::CpoUserActivityTotal[*];
}

Association client_portal::CpoClientOutstandingTotals
{
   outstandingTotal: client_portal::CpoClientOutstandingTotal[0..1];
   clientInvoiceViews: client_portal::CpoInvoiceView[*];
}

// ================================================================ associations, across a boundary
//
// SEVEN, reaching BOTH dependencies -- four into client-reporting, three into fee-billing.
// This is the construct the project exists to exercise: an association end puts a property on
// the DEPENDENCY'S class, so `client_reporting::CrpStatement` now carries `portalEntries` and
// `fee_billing::BilInvoice` now carries `portalView`, neither of which those projects declared.
// Both MANIFESTs were read before these names were chosen, because that namespace is shared
// and nobody owns it: client-reporting has already put `clientStatements`, `deliveries` and
// nine more onto its own classes, and a second project reusing one of those names is a
// collision that names the property and not the project.

// ---- into client-reporting -----------------------------------------------------------------
// FOR: the statement a catalogue row publishes. The join that makes the portal a portal.
Association client_portal::CpoEntryStatement
{
   statement: client_reporting::CrpStatement[0..1];
   portalEntries: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[*];
}

// FOR: the dispatch the same document went out on, so the hash served can be compared with the
// hash delivered.
Association client_portal::CpoEntryDelivery
{
   delivery: client_reporting::CrpDelivery[0..1];
   portalEntry: client_portal::CpoDocumentEntry[0..1];
}

// FOR: the costs disclosure behind an acknowledgement request -- the commonest thing a client
// is actually made to accept.
Association client_portal::CpoRequestDisclosure
{
   disclosure: client_reporting::CrpCostsDisclosure[0..1];
   disclosureRequests: client_portal::CpoAcknowledgementRequest[*];
}

// FOR: the ten-percent depreciation notice an alert is raising. The deadline lives upstream on
// the notice; the portal only has to show it before that date.
Association client_portal::CpoAlertNotice
{
   notice: client_reporting::CrpDepreciationNotice[0..1];
   portalAlerts: client_portal::CpoAlert[*];
}

// ---- into fee-billing ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// FOR: the invoice behind what the screen shows. One portal row per invoice.
Association client_portal::CpoInvoiceViewInvoice
{
   invoice: fee_billing::BilInvoice[0..1];
   portalView: client_portal::CpoInvoiceView[0..1];
}

// FOR: reconciling an instruction straight against the biller, without going through this
// project's own row.
Association client_portal::CpoInstructionInvoice
{
   instructedInvoice: fee_billing::BilInvoice[0..1];
   invoiceInstructions: client_portal::CpoPaymentInstruction[*];
}

// FOR: telling the client what became of the charge they queried. The dispute is fee-billing's;
// the telling is the portal's.
Association client_portal::CpoAlertDispute
{
   dispute: fee_billing::BilDispute[0..1];
   disputeAlerts: client_portal::CpoAlert[*];
}
